'''Robert Howard Sand''' (born August 12, 1982)<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/489138/Robert_Howard_Sand.html| title= Rob Sand| website= LegiStorm| access-date= October 5, 2026}}</ref> is an American lawyer and politician who has served since 2019 as the 33rd [[Iowa Auditor of State|state auditor of Iowa]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he was the assistant [[Iowa Attorney General|attorney general of Iowa]] from 2010 to 2017. In 2018, he ran for state auditor, defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Mary Mosiman]]. After considering a run for [[governor of Iowa]] in 2022, Sand opted to run for reelection and was the only Democrat elected to a statewide office in Iowa in [[2022 Iowa State Auditor election|2022]]. 

In May 2025, he announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Iowa gubernatorial election]]. He is the Democratic nominee after no other Democrats qualified, and faces Republican businessman and farmer [[Zach Lahn]].<ref name= "announcement">{{Cite web |last= Tallal |first= Skylar |date= 2025-05-12 |title= Iowa Auditor Rob Sand announces 2026 gubernatorial run |url= https://cbs2iowa.com/news/local/iowa-auditor-rob-sand-announces-2026-gubernatorial-run-governor-kim-reynolds-republicans-democrats |access-date= 2025-05-12 |website= [[KGAN]] |language= en}}</ref><ref name= "announcement1">{{Cite web |last= Fingerhut| first= Hannah| date= May 12, 2025| title= Iowa's lone Democratic statewide officeholder Rob Sand announces 2026 bid for governor| url= https://apnews.com/article/iowa-governor-2026-election-democrat-rob-sand-98064557cfa2c5ba290e48f0d5799a4e| access-date=May 30, 2025 |website= [[AP News]]| language= en}}</ref><ref name= "announcement2">{{Cite web |last= Maricle| first= Kelly| date= May 12, 2025| title= Rob Sand announces run for Iowa governor| url= https://who13.com/news/rob-sand-announces-run-for-iowa-governor/ |access-date= May 30, 2025 |website= [[WHO-DT|WHO 13]]| language= en}}</ref><ref name= "announcement3">{{Cite web| last1= Luu| first1= Isabella| last2= Sostaric| first2= Katarina| last3= Gerlock| first3= Grant| date= May 12, 2025| title= Democrat Rob Sand jumps into the race for Iowa governor| url= https://www.iowapublicradio.org/2025-05-12/democrat-rob-sand-jumps-into-the-race-for-iowa-governor| access-date= May 30, 2025 |website= [[Iowa Public Radio]]| language= en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |work= KCCI |title= Democrat Julie Stauch exits race for Iowa governor after petition issues |url= https://www.kcci.com/article/iowa-politics-democrat-julie-stauch-exits-iowa-governor-race-after-petition-issues/70741463 |date= March 14, 2026 |access-date= March 15, 2026}}</ref>

==Early life==
Sand was born in [[Decorah, Iowa]].<ref name= "AboutAuditor">{{cite web| url= https://www.auditor.iowa.gov/about-us |title= Auditor of State Rob Sand| access-date= June 26, 2025}}</ref><ref name= "IOR">{{cite book |title= Iowa Official Register (2023-2024) |url= https://publications.iowa.gov/50100/1/Iowa_official_register_2023-24_OCR_.pdf}}</ref> His mother, Leslie, worked as a physical therapist and his father, Kevin, was a doctor. While he attended [[Decorah High School]], he spent two years lobbying community leaders in Decorah to build a [[skate park]].<ref name= whitecollar/><ref>{{cite web| last= Scull |first= Sarah |url= https://www.crestonnews.com/news/local/2021/08/12/state-auditor-speaks-on-pie-partisanship-and-public-trust/| title= State auditor speaks on P.I.E, partisanship and public trust |work= Creston News Advertiser |date= 2021-08-12 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> Sand graduated from Decorah High School in 2001.<ref name= "AboutAuditor"/><ref name= "IOR"/><ref name= whitecollar/> He enrolled at [[Brown University]], where he was awarded a [[Harry S. Truman Scholarship]].<ref name= "AboutAuditor"/><ref name= nyt>{{cite news |last1= Ember |first1= Sydney |title= The Bowhunting Influencer of Iowa Democrats |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2019/12/07/us/politics/iowa-democrats-2020.html |access-date= 25 June 2025 |publisher= The New York Times |date= December 7, 2019}}</ref>

While in college, Sand spent a semester interning in the Washington, D.C., office of [[United States Senate|Senator]] [[Tom Harkin]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= Brennan |first1= Paul |title= Rob Sand, the last remaining Democrat in statewide office, announces his run for governor |url= https://littlevillagemag.com/rob-sand-announces-run-for-governor/ |access-date= 25 June 2025 |work= Little Village |date= 12 May 2025}}</ref> He made his first political donation to [[Howard Dean]]'s [[Howard Dean 2004 presidential campaign|2004 presidential campaign]], but missed the [[2004 Iowa Democratic presidential caucuses|Iowa caucus]] because he was modeling menswear in Milan.<ref name= newsweek>{{cite web| last= Romano |first= Andrew |url= https://www.newsweek.com/can-youth-vote-save-obama-103621 |title= Can the Youth Vote Save Obama? |publisher= Newsweek |date= 2004-01-19 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> He also modeled in Paris.<ref name= nyt/> Sand graduated from Brown with a [[bachelor's degree]] in political science in 2005.<ref name= whitecollar/> He caucused for [[Barack Obama]]'s [[Barack Obama 2008 presidential campaign|presidential campaign]] in [[2008 Iowa Democratic presidential caucuses|2008]].<ref name= newsweek/> He was admitted to Harvard Law School before enrolling at the [[University of Iowa College of Law]], earning his [[Juris Doctor]] in 2010.<ref name="IOR" /><ref name="whitecollar" /><ref>{{cite web| url= https://decorahnewspapers.com/Content/Home/Home/Article/Decorah-native-State-Auditor-Rob-Sand-hosts-series-of-town-hall-meetings/-2/-2/48618| title= Decorah native State Auditor Rob Sand hosts series of town hall meetings |website= decorahnewspapers.com| access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Slevin |first=Peter |date=2026-09-21 |title=A Populist Revival in Iowa |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/the-lede/a-populist-revival-in-iowa |access-date=2026-09-26 |work=The New Yorker |language=en-US |issn=0028-792X}}</ref> In law school, he worked as a law clerk for the Office of the Attorney General in Des Moines, and was a summer law clerk at Simmons Perrine Moyer Bergman PLC in [[Cedar Rapids, Iowa]].<ref>{{cite web| url= https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/179237/rob-sand |title= The Voter's Self Defense System}}</ref>{{better source needed|date=June 2025}} He was the editor-in-chief of ''[[The Journal of Corporation Law]].''<ref>{{Cite web |title= Secure Request and Redirect |url= https://heinonline.org/HOL/Page?handle=hein.journals/jcorl35&id=621&collection=journals |access-date= 2026-06-09 |website= heinonline.org}}</ref>{{Primary source inline| date= June 2026}}

== Political career==
=== Early career ===
In 2006, Sand worked as an aide for [[Iowa House of Representatives|State Representative]] [[Ed Fallon]]'s campaign for [[governor of Iowa]]. After Fallon lost the [[Partisan primary|primary election]], Sand became the [[campaign manager]] for Denise O'Brien, the Democratic nominee for [[Iowa Secretary of Agriculture]].<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.newspapers.com/image/544070255/?terms=%22rob%20sand%22&match=1 |title= 24 Oct 2006, 10 - The Muscatine Journal |publisher= Newspapers.com |date= 2006-10-24 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.newspapers.com/image/391124840/?terms=%22rob%20sand%22&match=1 |title= 3 Nov 2006, 4| work= Globe-Gazette |via= Newspapers.com |date= 2006-11-03 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> While he was on the campaign trail, he met [[Tom Miller (politician)|Tom J. Miller]], who was [[Iowa Attorney General]] at the time.<ref name= whitecollar>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/2015/12/20/des-moines-prosecutor-keeps-eye-white-collar-crime/77031686/ |title= Meet the man who battles white-collar crime |first= Grant|last= Rodgers| work= The Des Moines Register |date= 2015-12-20 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> 

=== Attorney General Office ===
Miller appointed Sand assistant attorney general in September 2010.<ref name=run/> Upon joining the attorney general's office, he became the lead prosecutor for the Iowa Film Office tax credit scandal, in which millions of dollars of film tax credits were fraudulently obtained.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://wcfcourier.com/news/local/dept-head-testifies-at-trial-for-former-iowa-film-office/article_e4d15298-da3b-59a2-9f85-ad45cd865951.html |title= Dept. head testifies at trial for former Iowa Film Office manager |work= Waterloo-Cedar Falls Courier |date= August 25, 2011| access-date= May 13, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.iowaattorneygeneral.gov/newsroom/judge-sentences-producer-to-10-years-in-prison-for-iowa-film-tax-credit-fraud |title= Judge Sentences Producer to 10 Years in Prison for Iowa Film Tax Credit Fraud |publisher= iowaattorneygeneral.gov |date= May 31, 2012 |access-date= May 13, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/crime-and-courts/2017/05/11/filmmaker-acquitted-fraud-charge-tax-credit-scandal/318530001/ |title= Filmmaker acquitted on fraud charge in tax credit scandal |publisher= dmregister.com |date= May 11, 2017 |access-date= May 13, 2020 }}</ref> Miller then recruited Sand to work in the Area Prosecutions Division, which focuses on white-collar financial crime. Sand prosecuted an investment adviser for deceiving people to invest into the International Bank of Meekamui, an international [[Ponzi scheme]] emanating from [[Papua New Guinea]].<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.newtondailynews.com/2017/03/16/money-adviser-gets-20-years-in-prison-for-investment-scam/arjiz2v/ |title= Money adviser gets 20 years in prison for investment scam |work= Newton Daily News| date= 2017-03-16 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref>

Sand also prosecuted the $25 million [[Hot Lotto fraud scandal]], in which seven lottery tickets across five states were rigged. Eddie Tipton, the former information security director of the [[Multi-State Lottery Association]], confessed to rigging a random number generator in the largest lottery rigging scheme in US history.<ref name= nytmagazine>{{cite news| url= https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/05/03/magazine/money-issue-iowa-lottery-fraud-mystery.html |title= The Man Who Cracked the Lottery |work= [[The New York Times]] |first= Reid| last= Forgrave| date= May 3, 2018 |access-date= November 16, 2018}}</ref> Tipton was sentenced to 25 years in prison in 2017.<ref name= book>{{cite web| url= https://www.axios.com/local/des-moines/2022/04/27/iowa-auditor-rob-sand-new-book-winning-ticket| title= Lottery scam detailed in Iowa auditor's new book| first= Jason| last= Clayworth| date= April 27, 2022| website= Axios| access-date= August 19, 2022}}</ref> After completing the prosecution, Sand resigned from the Attorney General's office so that he could run for office.<ref name= nytmagazine/>

=== State Auditor ===
[[File:Rob sand IA.png|thumb|Rob Sand in 2019]]
In the [[2018 Iowa elections#Auditor|2018 elections]], Sand ran for [[Iowa State Auditor]].<ref name=run>{{cite web|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/2017/11/13/democrat-rob-sand-run-iowa-auditor-2018/858831001/ |title=Democrat Rob Sand will run for Iowa auditor in 2018 |work=The Des Moines Register |date=November 13, 2017 |access-date=November 16, 2018}}</ref> He was not opposed in the Democratic Party primary election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/438776728/?terms=%22rob%20sand%22&match=1 |title=The Des Moines Register 05 Jun 2018, page A7 |publisher=Newspapers.com |date=2018-06-05 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref> Sand defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Mary Mosiman]], the incumbent, in the general election,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/11/05/iowa-election-2018-results-auditor-mary-mosiman-rob-sand-democrat-republican-lottery-vote-cpa-audit/1732370002/ |title=Iowa election 2018 results: Rob Sand defeats Mary Mosiman for state auditor |work=The Des Moines Register|date=2018-11-05 |access-date=November 16, 2018}}</ref> 51% to 46%, while [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] Fred Perryman received 3% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wqad.com/article/news/politics/heres-whos-winning-the-races-for-iowas-state-offices/526-1ff71189-b3fe-4616-8b43-63567ee345bc |title=Here's who won the races for Iowa's state offices |publisher=wqad.com |date=2018-11-06 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://decorahnewspapers.com/Content/Home/Home/Article/Decorah-native-Sand-elected-state-auditor/-2/-2/46584 |title=Decorah native Sand elected state auditor |publisher=Decorah Newspapers |date=2018-11-07 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref> He became the fourth Democrat elected Iowa State Auditor, and just the second in the past 100 years.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kcrg.com/content/news/Rob-Sand-is-second-Democratic-auditor-in-over-100-years-503806481.html|title=Rob Sand is second Democratic auditor in over 100 years|website=KCRG|date=January 2, 2019 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref> 

Sand was sworn into office on January 2, 2019. At his swearing-in ceremony, he announced that he would have a "tri-partisan" leadership team, with his top three assistants being a Democrat, a Republican, and an [[independent politician|independent]].<ref>{{cite web| url= https://decorahnewspapers.com/Content/Home/Home/Article/Decorah-native-Rob-Sand-takes-oath-of-office-announces-tri-partisan-leadership-team/-2/-2/46909 |title= Decorah native Rob Sand takes oath of office, announces 'tri-partisan' leadership team |publisher= Decorah Newspapers |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> As state auditor, Sand developed the Public Innovations and Efficiencies (PIE) program, which awards fiscal efficiency in government.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://nonpareilonline.com/news/local/state-auditor-sand-talks-saving-taxpayer-money-and-pie-during-sunset-park-visit/article_59a55fb2-0de8-11ed-9bed-9bfc37bf2cd3.html| title= State Auditor Sand talks saving taxpayer money and PIE during Sunset Park visit| first= David |last= Golbitz| work= The Daily Nonpareil |date= July 28, 2022 |location= Council Bluffs, Iowa| access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> The PIE program inspired [[Shad White]], the Republican [[state auditor of Mississippi]], to develop a similar program.<ref>{{cite web| last= White| first= Shad |url= https://www.vicksburgpost.com/2022/02/02/guest-column-new-award-honors-good-efficient-government/ |title= GUEST COLUMN: New Award Honors Good, Efficient Government |work= The Vicksburg Post |date= 2022-02-02 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.oskaloosa.com/news/local_news/sand-i-m-interested-in-public-service/article_7d80f21c-bb62-11ec-89d7-a332f22e6ab7.html| title= Sand: 'I'm interested in public service.'| first= Emily| last= Hawk| work= Oskaloosa Herald| date= April 14, 2022| access-date= August 19, 2022}}</ref> Before the [[2020 Iowa Democratic presidential caucuses|2020 presidential caucuses]], multiple candidates sought Sand's endorsement.<ref>{{cite web| last= Lowry |first= Rich |url= https://www.politico.com/story/2018/09/17/rob-sand-iowa-robert-mueller-826314 |title= Top Democrats flock to Iowa to help 'young Robert Mueller' |publisher= Politico |date= September 17, 2018 |access-date= November 16, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| first= Clay| last= Masters |url= https://www.npr.org/2020/01/12/795203957/why-the-most-coveted-democratic-endorser-in-iowa-isnt-picking-sides |title= Why The Most Coveted Democratic Endorser In Iowa Isn't Picking Sides |publisher= NPR |date= 2020-01-12 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref> He did not endorse a candidate, but he did co-host a campaign event in Decorah with [[Cory Booker]] that he said did not constitute an endorsement.

After considering a campaign for governor of Iowa against [[Kim Reynolds]],<ref>{{cite web| last= Kauffman |first= Clark |url= https://www.thegazette.com/government-politics/rob-sand-says-hes-praying-for-guidance-whether-to-run-for-iowa-governor/ |title= Rob Sand says he's praying for guidance whether to run for Iowa governor |publisher= The Gazette |date= 2021-06-14 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> Sand announced in December 2021 that he would run for reelection in 2022.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/12/07/democrat-rob-sand-seeking-re-election-state-auditor-wont-run-iowa-governor-race-2022/6423059001/ |title= Democrat Rob Sand to seek reelection as Iowa state auditor, won't run for governor in 2022| first= Stephen| last= Gruber-Miller| work= The Des Moines Register| access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> In January 2022, [[Mary Ann Hanusa]], a Republican former state representative, announced that she would run for state auditor.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2022/01/05/republican-mary-ann-hanusa-run-against-democrat-rob-sand-iowa-auditor-election-2022/9096116002/ |title= Republican Mary Ann Hanusa to run against Democrat Rob Sand |first= Brianne |last= Pfannenstiel| work= The Des Moines Register |date= 2022-01-05 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> Todd Halbur, a small business owner, defeated Hanusa in the primary election<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2022/06/07/iowa-state-auditor-primary-election-results-2022-winner/7537970001/ |title= Republican Iowa auditor race goes to Todd Halbur| first= Tyler| last= Jett |work= The Des Moines Register |date= 2022-06-07 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> and Sand defeated Halbur in the November 8 general election with 50.1% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2022/11/18/todd-halbur-concedes-rob-sand-iowa-state-auditor-election-2022/69651993007/| title= Todd Halbur concedes to Rob Sand in Iowa auditor's race| work= The Des Moines Register| first= Stephen| last= Gruber-Miller| date= November 18, 2022| access-date= November 20, 2022}}</ref>

Senate File 478, a bill that would restrict the powers of the State Auditor, passed the Iowa Legislature in April 2023 and was signed by Governor Reynolds in June.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2023/06/01/gov-kim-reynolds-approves-final-bills-from-the-2023-legislative-session/ | title= Gov. Kim Reynolds approves final bills from the 2023 legislative session |date= June 2, 2023 }}</ref> Sand has held multiple press conferences and town halls across Iowa in opposition to the bill, claiming it is targeting him as Iowa's only remaining statewide elected Democrat.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.thegazette.com/state-government/iowa-auditor-rob-sand-barnstorms-against-bill-that-will-restrict-his-watchdog-role/ |title= What does Iowa's taxpayer watchdog lose from new bill? And why he's campaigning to stop it }}</ref><ref name= "wapo-sf478">{{Cite news |last= Wang |first= Amy B. |date= 2023-04-29 |title= Iowa legislature passes bill restricting state auditor's access to records |language= en-US |newspaper= Washington Post |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/04/27/iowa-state-legislator-auditor-republicans/ |access-date= 2023-07-15 |issn= 0190-8286}}</ref> The bill, which was opposed by all Democrats in the Iowa House and Senate along with six House Republicans,<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2023/04/20/iowa-house-passes-bill-limiting-state-auditor-rob-sands-powers/70134933007/ | title= Iowa House votes to restrict state auditor's access to information, ability to go to court | website= [[The Des Moines Register]] }}</ref> has been opposed by the National State Auditors Association, the American Institute of CPAs, and former [[Comptroller General of the United States|U.S. Comptroller General]] [[David M. Walker (U.S. Comptroller General)|David Walker]].<ref name= "wapo-sf478" />

==== Investigations and audits ==== 

From 2011 to 2019, Jeffrey Dworek, a Metro Waste Authority (MWA) director of operations, defrauded the company of over $2 million by billing services not rendered to subcontractors he owned.<ref name= "MWA">{{cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/state-auditor-details-investigation-of-2m-in-disbursements-at-metro-waste/30284696|title=State auditor details $2M in 'unsupported, 'improper' disbursements at Metro Waste|date= December 19, 2019|access-date=June 26, 2025}}</ref> He sent money to Lemar Programming Company, International Telemetry Technologies, and Britad Enterprises and deposited it in his own accounts.<ref name= "MWA"/> On January 8, 2021, he was sentenced to 4.75 years in federal prison on charges of mail fraud by Chief District Judge [[John Alfred Jarvey]].<ref name= "Feds">{{cite web|url=https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdia/pr/jeffrey-dworek-sentenced-prison-defrauding-metro-waste-authority|title=Jeffrey Dworek Sentenced to Prison for Defrauding Metro Waste Authority|date= January 11, 2021|access-date=June 26, 2025}}</ref> This was a joint investigation with the Auditor and the [[FBI]].<ref name= "Feds"/> He was prosecuted by [[United States Attorney for the Southern District of Iowa]] Richard D. Westphal.<ref name= "Feds"/>

From 2019 to 2025, the Auditor's office investigated the [[Johnson County, Iowa|Johnson County]] Conservation Board due to misspent $95,000 by former Conservation Director Larry Gullett and Operations Superintendent Wade Schultz.<ref name= "Johnson">{{cite web| url= https://cbs2iowa.com/news/local/johnson-county-responds-to-state-auditors-investigation-into-conservation-department| title= Johnson County responds to state auditor's investigation into conservation department| date= March 4, 2025 |access-date= June 26, 2025}}</ref> The Johnson County Conservation Board requested the investigation after finding the mismanagement.<ref name= "Johnson"/>

In 2020, an audit conducted by Sand's office found that Governor [[Kim Reynolds]] misspent $21&nbsp;million from the [[CARES Act]] on unrelated state contracts rather than on managing the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Iowa|COVID-19 pandemic]]. The state returned the money to their coronavirus relief fund.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.kcci.com/article/reynolds-state-will-return-dollar21-million-in-cares-act-funding-used-on-it-infrastructure/34966747# |title= Iowa to return $21 million in CARES funding used on IT infrastructure |publisher= Kcci.com |date= 2020-12-14 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref> Sand alleged Reynolds broke Iowa's law against self-promotion by appearing in a taxpayer-funded video to promote [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|wearing a mask]]. An Iowa ethics board later found Reynolds did not break any laws.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/06/03/auditor-rob-sand-says-kim-reynolds-broke-iowa-self-promotion-law-mask-video-covid-disaster-exemption/7517086002/ |title= Auditor alleges Gov. Reynolds broke self-promotion law with COVID video |work= The Des Moines Register |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref> In November 2021, Sand said Reynolds improperly used nearly $450,000 in federal coronavirus relief funds to pay salaries for 21 staff members for three months in the last year.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/11/15/auditor-rob-sands-says-iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-abused-covid-money/8634077002/ |title= Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds abused COVID money, State Auditor Rob Sand says |work= The Des Moines Register |date= 2021-11-15 |access-date= 2022-08-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2022/03/01/kim-reynolds-should-repay-450-k-federal-covid-relief-rob-sand-says/6979441001/ |title= Kim Reynolds should repay $450K in federal COVID relief, Rob Sand says |work= The Des Moines Register |date= 2022-03-01 |access-date=2022-08-19}}</ref>

In 2022 and 2023, an audit was conducted of the Iowa Judicial Branch, which found $27.6 million was sent to the wrong place and shorted state and local funds.<ref name= "Judicial">{{cite web| url= https://www.iowapublicradio.org/state-government-news/2024-12-18/judicial-branch-audit-finds-millions-went-to-wrong-state-funds-due-to-it-errors| title= Judicial branch audit says $27.6 million went to wrong state funds due to IT errors| publisher= [[Iowa Public Radio]]| last= Sostaric| first= Katarina| date= December 18, 2024| access-date= June 26, 2025}}</ref> This was caused by an IT error and was fixed but was investigated by the Auditor.<ref name= "Judicial"/> 

In 2024, an investigation took place at the University of Iowa's Department of Physics and Astronomy's Machine Shop after Brian Busch deposited $943,634.97 into his personal account instead of the university's accounts.<ref name= "IowaUni">{{cite web|url=https://www.kcrg.com/2024/09/04/ui-machine-shop-manager-accused-diverting-nearly-950000/|title=University of Iowa employee accused of pocketing nearly $1 million|publisher= [[KCRG-TV|KCRG]]|last=Hendricks|first=Conner|date=September 4, 2024|access-date=June 27, 2025}}</ref><ref name= "Arrest">{{cite web|url=https://www.kcrg.com/2024/09/12/police-arrest-former-univ-iowa-machine-shop-manager-accused-pocketing-nearly-1-million/|title=Police arrest former Univ. of Iowa Machine Shop manager accused of pocketing nearly $1 million|publisher= [[KCRG-TV|KCRG]]|date=September 12, 2024|access-date=June 27, 2025}}</ref> It was also revealed that he possibly diverted money from the university to his own business.<ref name= "IowaUni"/><ref name= "Arrest"/> Busch was fired after being placed on paid administrative leave from 2021 to 2024.<ref name= "IowaUni"/><ref name= "Arrest"/> He was then arrested on charges of "First Degree Theft, Tampering with Records, Ongoing Criminal Conduct and First Degree Fraudulent Practice."<ref name= "Arrest"/>

In January 2025, Sand concluded an investigation that found over $425,000 was misspent, from 2015 to 2022, by Children and Families of Iowa administrator Jodi Spargur-Tate, with $324,586 taken by her directly and $100,000 distributed to her family.<ref name="ICD">{{cite web|last=Kauffman|first=Clark|url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2025/01/30/auditor-more-than-400k-in-public-money-misspent-through-state-run-program/|title=Auditor: More than $400K in public money misspent through state-run program|publisher=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]]|access-date=June 26, 2025|date= January 30, 2025}}</ref><ref name="CFI">{{cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/jodi-spargur-tate-iowa-cfi-audit-rob-sand/63623411|title=Former Children and Families of Iowa program director misspent over $400k, audit finds|publisher=[[KCCI]]|access-date=June 26, 2025|date= January 30, 2025}}</ref> The Children and Families of Iowa is funded by the [[United States Department of Labor]] and administered by [[Iowa Workforce Development]] (IWD).<ref name="ICD"/><ref name="CFI"/> IWD asked the auditor's office to initiate the special investigation, after initially being investigated by Children and Families of Iowa.<ref name="ICD"/><ref name="CFI"/> No charges have been filed, but the findings were given to the Polk County Sheriff’s Office, [[Iowa Division of Criminal Investigation]], the Polk County Attorney’s Office, and the [[Iowa Attorney General]]’s Office.<ref name="ICD"/><ref name="CFI"/>

In May 2025, [[Conesville, Iowa|Conesville]] Mayor Tom Van Auken and City Clerk Carol Zuniga raised concerns about a former clerk, Yamira Martinez, misusing $127,000 and asked that the [[Muscatine County, Iowa|Muscatine County Sheriff's Office]] investigate.<ref name= "conesville">{{cite web|last=Hotle|first=David|url=https://www.thegazette.com/crime-courts/auditor-discovers-127k-in-misspent-funds-in-conesville-former-city-clerk-arrested/|title=Auditor discovers $127K in misspent funds in Conesville; former city clerk arrested|publisher=[[Muscatine Journal]]|access-date=June 26, 2025|date= June 25, 2025}}</ref><ref name= "conesville2">{{cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/conesville-iowa-yamira-martinez-investigation-clerk-127k-misused-funds/65173473|title=Iowa auditor's office finds more than $127K in misused funds, improper disbursements by city clerk|publisher=[[KCCI]]|access-date=June 26, 2025|date= June 24, 2025}}</ref> After the Sheriff began investigating, the city additionally asked for an audit from the State Auditor.<ref name= "conesville"/><ref name= "conesville2"/> In June 2025, Sand concluded that investigation.<ref name= "conesville"/><ref name= "conesville2"/> Martinez was arrested by the Muscatine County Sheriff's Office for "two counts of ongoing criminal conduct, two counts of theft, and two counts related to forgery and fraud."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kcrg.com/2025/06/25/former-city-clerk-conesville-arrested-following-special-investigation-state-auditor/|title=Former city clerk of Conesville arrested following special investigation from Auditor of State|publisher=[[KCRG-TV|KCRG]]|access-date=June 27, 2025|date=June 25, 2025}}</ref>

=== 2026 Iowa gubernatorial race ===
{{main|2026 Iowa gubernatorial election}}
[[File:Rob Sand campaigning in Clay County September 2025.jpg|thumb|Sand speaking at his campaign rally in [[Clay County, Iowa]], September 2025]]

On May 12, 2025, Sand announced his candidacy for the Democratic nomination in the [[2026 Iowa gubernatorial election]].<ref name= "announcement"/><ref name= "announcement1"/><ref name= "announcement2"/><ref name= "announcement3"/> 

He raised $8 million in 2024, $3 million of which came from his wife Christine and $4 million from her parents, Nixon and Nancy Lauridsen.<ref name= "announcement1"/><ref name= "announcement2"/><ref name= "announcement3"/><ref name= "announcement4">{{Cite web|last=Smith|first=Zachary Oren|date=May 13, 2025|title=Rob Sand’s governor campaign raises $2.25 million in first 24 hours|url=https://iowastartingline.com/2025/05/13/rob-sand-record-fundraising-governor-reynolds/|access-date=May 30, 2025 |website=Iowa Starting Line|language=en}}</ref> The last $1 million came from 28,000 individual donations.<ref name= "announcement4"/> In the first 24 hours of his campaign, he raised over $2 million from individual donations.<ref name= "announcement4"/> Throughout his campaign, Sand has positioned himself as an "authentic" Iowan by touting his fondness for [[hunting]], while also emphasizing the importance of "commonsense" reform, pointing to a declining economy, exodus of college-educated students, and a rapid cancer growth rate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/21/iowa-democrats-rob-sand-midterms-hunting-00837624 |first=Adam |last=Wren |website=[[Politico]] |date=March 21, 2026 |title='I’m super-Republican, but you got my vote': The Democratic deer hunter setting his sights on flipping Iowa |access-date=May 20, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/08/us/rob-sand-iowa-governor-democrat.html |first=Julie |last=Bosman |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=May 8, 2026 |title=The Democrat Making Republicans Nervous in Iowa |access-date=May 20, 2026}}</ref>

On June 16, 2025, Sand announced a "100 Town Hall Tour", holding events in all 99 counties in Iowa plus a second one in [[Lee County, Iowa|Lee County]], as Lee County has dual county seats of [[Keokuk, Iowa|Keokuk]] and [[Fort Madison, Iowa|Fort Madison]].<ref name= "KCCI">{{Cite web|last1=Tyler|first1=Olivia|last2=Rooker|first2=Amanda|date=June 16, 2025|title=Democratic candidate for governor Rob Sand launches '100 Town Hall Tour' across Iowa|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/democratic-candidate-for-governor-rob-sand-launches-100-town-hall-tour-across-iowa/65058879|access-date=June 17, 2025 |website=[[KCCI]]}}</ref> At a town hall attended by nearly 1,000 people in [[Iowa City, Iowa|Iowa City]], Sand said he would veto all "culture war"-related bills, but said he supported Iowa's recently implemented law banning transgender athletes from women's sports.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Halawith |first= Liam |title= Rob Sand's transgender stance met with silence in Iowa City town hall |url= https://www.press-citizen.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/09/15/democrat-rob-sand-supports-iowa-transgender-athlete-ban-iowa-governor-race-2026/91769643007/ |access-date= 2026-09-17 |website= Iowa City Press-Citizen |language= en-US}}</ref>

Sand will face Republican nominee [[Zach Lahn]] in the November general election.

On October 19, 2026, Sand withdrew from an Iowa governorship debate that was scheduled for October 21 after his brother-in-law was killed in a car crash.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Rob Sand withdraws from Iowa governor debate after brother-in-law's death|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/rob-sand-withdraws-from-iowa-governor-debate-after-brother-in-laws-death/74186241|date=October 19, 2026|website=KCCI}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Sand and his wife, Christine Lauridsen, met in Des Moines and married in [[Madison County, Iowa|Madison County]], on June 2, 2012, and have two sons.<ref name= "AboutAuditor"/><ref name= "IOR"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://robsand.com/about/|title= Meet Rob Sand|access-date= June 26, 2025}}</ref> In October 2026, Sand's brother-in-law, Walter E. Lauridsen II, was killed in [[Lucas County, Iowa|Lucas County]] when he was struck by a vehicle after walking into the lane of traffic.<ref>{{citeweb|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/des-moines-man-killed-in-southern-iowa-crash-walter-e-lauridsen-ii/74157575|title=Walter Lauridsen, brother-in-law of governor candidate Rob Sand, killed in southern Iowa crash|publisher=[[KCCI]]|date=October 16, 2026|access-date=October 18, 2026}}</ref>

Christine is the CEO of her family's business, Lauridsen Group.<ref name="Lauridsen">{{cite web|url=https://lauridsengroupinc.com/|title= The Lauridsen Group, Inc.|access-date=June 26, 2025}}</ref> 

Sand wrote ''The Winning Ticket: Uncovering America's Biggest Lottery Scam'', a book about the [[Hot Lotto fraud scandal]], with Reid Forgrave, a reporter for ''[[The Des Moines Register]]''. The book was published on May 1, 2022.<ref name=book/>

